WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.070
Good morning John.

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:07.180
VidCon is in a week, which, as per tradition,
means that I am experiencing a great deal

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:08.440
of general, non-specific anxiety.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.889
Usually, for me, my anxiety comes from a place
and I can say "Okay, there is a problem that

00:00:11.889 --> 00:00:14.719
I have to solve, and then hopefully it will
solve the problem and then it will go away."

00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:15.740
VidCon is not like that.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.530
I'm just anxious, and then it's over, and
I just have to wait it out.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.980
The only thing I can do is distract myself,
which is why I've written 4 Vlogbrothers scripts

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.859
in the last week, none of which I'm using
right now.

00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:28.859
I did try to use them, but I felt dishonest
talking about anything except for the thing

00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:30.230
that I'm actually freaking out about right
now.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:34.050
Which is specifically VidCon, and because
I'm a professional over-analyzer of things,

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:38.300
whether VidCon, and less specifically online
video, and even less specifically the internet,

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.890
is a good thing for humanity.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:42.040
And this isn't usually a complicated question
for me.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.660
The internet leads to more connections between
people, creating more cultural and creative

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:50.140
diversity and it increases everyone's access
dramatically to more information and more

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.140
free tools.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:54.160
So it seems to me that the internet has had
a very positive effect on the world.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.449
But there are also things I dislike about
the internet.

00:00:56.449 --> 00:01:02.320
I dislike that the internet's focus on the
very, very cheap and the free means that in

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.630
order for platforms to make money, they have
to consolidate a huge amount of culture in

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:10.560
one place and have platforms that have hundreds
of millions of people on them and that this,

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.880
in turn, means that there is this massive
economy of scale which then means that only

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:20.759
a few people get very, very rich and they
end up employing just regular middle class

00:01:20.759 --> 00:01:21.841
people not at all.

00:01:21.841 --> 00:01:24.770
There are also cultural effects that I am
ambivalent about.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:29.770
The recent user revolt at Reddit over the
site's interest in not supporting hateful

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.580
and actively abusive communities confuses
me.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:37.220
And the internet is extremely effective at
creating pockets of bias and misinformation

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.840
that lead to the dehumanization of the opposition.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.560
But, I think that the internet can also make
people more tolerant of other perspectives

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.649
just by making the existence of those other
perspectives more visible.

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:52.040
And whatever the thing is that we used to
call "fame" that I'm uncomfortable calling

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:55.020
fame now, has started to seem really weird
to me.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:59.510
This is probably because I now, to some extent,
actually experience the effects of this "thing."

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:03.620
But one of the really great and exciting things
about online success has always been to me

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:10.320
that that success comes along with the creation
of a community and a real relationship between

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.500
a creator and the people consuming the content
they create.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:19.400
But as this culture has grown, that relationship
starts to feel less real, and maybe less important,

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.650
and maybe like it doesn't even really exist.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.450
And thus, online success has started to look
a lot more like traditional fame.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:30.850
Online culture is becoming mainstream culture
and I had to learn early on that VidCon wasn't

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.910
just going to be affected by that, VidCon
was going to be part of that.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:38.590
I don't necessarily want online new media
to become mainstream media, but it's a thing

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:39.590
that's happening.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:42.780
This year at VidCon, Katie Couric is interviewing
Ze Frank.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:43.830
That's so...

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:44.830
Yeah.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:48.780
Now, with the Vlogbrothers, we can actually
sort of actively de-mainstreamify ourselves

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:55.570
by just making like sort of more off the cuff,
weird content that isn't going to be as popular

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:57.720
but will still be rewarding for the existing
community.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.060
But VidCon is a lot bigger than us and that
means that VidCon is going to change, and

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.160
sometimes in ways that are gonna annoy me.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:07.230
I'm very lucky, and I take the responsibility
seriously, that I can steer the ship that

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.750
is VidCon if I really feel like I need to.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:14.090
But there is a current that that ship is in
and I can't, and more importantly shouldn't,

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:15.090
fight it.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:18.650
Because in the end, I am extremely proud of
VidCon and I am extremely proud of online

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:19.650
video.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.550
But if these last 8 years of being involved
in this stuff has taught me anything, it's

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:25.510
that you can't bottle up and control culture.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:29.430
But you can, and you should, ask the members
of that culture to consider its effects.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:32.550
The internet, I think for the first time in
a long time, gave a lot of people, a lot of

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:37.730
normal people, the feeling that they could
actually have a significant impact on their

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:39.220
culture; they could actually change things.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.340
My hope is that everybody leaves VidCon with
that understanding reinforced.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:47.510
That these media and these platforms, this
entirely unprecedented cultural phenomenon

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:52.250
is not just something that you experience,
it's something that we are all a part of.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:54.150
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:55.740
Oh god, VidCon.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:56.800
Oh god.

